# Proposal: AI-Augmented Architecture Review
 
**For:** *[Architecture Review Board / CTO / Head of Engineering]*
**From:** *[Name, Enterprise Architecture]*
**Date:** 2026-08-05
**Decision requested:** Approval of the process, and of a time-boxed pilot
**Supporting detail:** *AI-Augmented Architecture Review — Process Design*
 
---
 
## The problem
 
Our federated review process depends on human reviewers reading every proposal against a growing body of published architecture decisions. That does not scale, and it is failing in predictable ways:
 
- Reviewers cannot reliably recall which of *[N]* published decisions apply to a given proposal, so conformance checking is inconsistent between reviewers and between domains.
- Review has become a queue. Cycle time is *[X days]*, and teams increasingly proceed before advice arrives.
- Institutional memory is lost. The same questions are re-litigated because nobody can find what was decided last time.
- Non-conformance is discovered late — in production, or not at all.
## The proposal
 
Add an **AI advisory step to the existing review process**. Proposals are automatically assessed against our published architecture decisions, producing an evidence-backed findings report for the human reviewer.
 
The advisory step identifies which decisions actually apply, and reports where the proposal conforms, deviates, or needs a waiver — citing the specific decision clause and the specific evidence in the proposal for every finding.
 
**The advisory step advises. Humans decide.** It produces no approvals and blocks no work. It is one more voice owing advice under our existing process, so the governance model does not change — only the speed and consistency with which advice is available.
 
## The process
 
```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["Published architecture decisions"] -. applicable decisions .-> C
    B["Solution proposal submitted"] --> C["Automated triage and<br/>conformance advice"]
    C --> D["Human review at the appropriate tier<br/><b>decision authority sits here</b>"]
    D --> E["Decision recorded:<br/>approval, waiver, or amended standard"]
    E --> F["Continuous assurance:<br/>drift and waiver expiry"]
    F -. findings .-> A
```
 
Only the shaded step is new. Intake, review tiers, decision rights, and recording are unchanged.
 
## What it delivers
 
| Capability | Benefit |
|---|---|
| Automated conformance findings against published decisions | Consistent review regardless of which reviewer is assigned |
| Automated triage — recommends the review tier and identifies affected parties | Removes the routing bottleneck; stops proposals reaching the wrong forum |
| Precedent retrieval across past decisions | Ends re-litigation of settled questions |
| Pre-read generation for review sessions | Meetings start at the disagreements, not the recap |
| Deviation clustering across the portfolio | Reveals when a repeatedly-waived standard is simply wrong |
 
## What it deliberately does not do
 
- It does not approve, reject, or block anything.
- It does not judge trade-offs where legitimate values conflict — that is the reviewers' job.
- It does not replace the advice conversation between teams and architects.
- It does not weaken accountability: a named human reviewer owns every published finding set.
## Phased adoption
 
| Phase | Scope | Duration |
|---|---|---|
| **1 — Foundation** | Decision authors confirm the constraints their decisions impose; establish an accuracy baseline from past reviews | *[6 weeks]* |
| **2 — Pilot** | Advisory findings for *[one domain]*; measure against how our own reviewers assess the same proposals | *[8 weeks]* |
| **3 — Rollout** | Extend to all domains and to the standard intake path | *[Q_]* |
| **4 — Continuous assurance** | Detect drift between what was approved and what was built | *[Q_]* |
 
We propose committing to **Phases 1 and 2 only**, with continuation contingent on measured accuracy.
 
## What we need
 
- *[N]* people for *[N]* weeks *[plus architect time for constraint confirmation]*
- Access to the decision corpus, design repository, and system definitions
- Agreement on the accuracy threshold that permits continuation
- *[Budget estimate]*
## Principal risks
 
| Risk | Mitigation |
|---|---|
| Findings are noisy, so reviewers stop reading them | An agreed accuracy threshold, measured in the pilot, gates continuation |
| Reviewers over-trust a clean report and stop reading proposals | Reports always show what was checked, abstained on, and out of scope — never a bare pass |
| The process entrenches existing standards against better ideas | Any deviation may be escalated as a proposal to amend the standard |
| Accountability becomes ambiguous | A named human reviewer owns every published finding set |
| Confidential material handled inappropriately | Confidentiality and retention arrangements agreed before the pilot handles real proposals |
 
## How we will judge it
 
The pilot succeeds only if, measured against past human reviews:
 
- Deviation detection is accurate enough to act on, against a threshold agreed in advance.
- Review cycle time falls by *[target]*.
- Reviewers use it when they are not required to.
Accuracy will be assessed against the rate at which *our own reviewers agree with each other*, which is the realistic ceiling for any reviewer, human or otherwise.
 
## Decision requested
 
Approval of the process as described, and authorisation to proceed with Phases 1 and 2, returning to this board with pilot results before any wider rollout.
 
